  "faqTitle": "FAQ",
  "@faqTitle": {"description": "AppBar title of the FAQ screen."},

  "faqQ1": "What does Signet actually do?",
  "@faqQ1": {"description": "FAQ question 1 (expansion tile title)."},

  "faqA1": "Signet lets you confirm that a person calling, texting, or video-chatting you is who they say they are — even if they sound right, know biographical facts, and are asking for something urgent. You pair once, in person, with someone you trust. From then on, either of you can ask for a 4-word code that only the real paired device can produce.",
  "@faqA1": {"description": "FAQ answer 1."},

  "faqQ2": "What does \"verified\" actually prove?",
  "@faqQ2": {"description": "FAQ question 2."},

  "faqA2": "It proves the person on the other end has physical access to the phone you paired with, and that that phone hasn't been compromised. It does not prove their voice is real — a deepfake that can also compel someone to read a code off the real phone would still verify. Signet's job is to raise the attacker's cost from 'clone a voice' to 'also physically steal an unlocked phone.'",
  "@faqA2": {"description": "FAQ answer 2."},

  "faqQ3": "Why does the 4-word code keep changing?",
  "@faqQ3": {"description": "FAQ question 3."},

  "faqA3": "Each code is valid for 30 seconds. That way, even if an attacker records one code during a real call, they can't reuse it later. Signet accepts codes from the current window plus one before and one after, so a small clock mismatch or slow reader doesn't fail a legitimate verify.",
  "@faqA3": {"description": "FAQ answer 3."},

  "faqQ4": "What if the codes don't match?",
  "@faqQ4": {"description": "FAQ question 4."},

  "faqA4": "Treat it as a red flag. A real paired contact's code will almost always match on the first try. If it doesn't: hang up, reach the person through a separate channel you independently know (their known phone number, in person, a mutual friend), and confirm before acting on anything they asked for.",
  "@faqA4": {"description": "FAQ answer 4."},

  "faqQ5": "Can Signet see my pairings or my codes?",
  "@faqQ5": {"description": "FAQ question 5."},

  "faqA5": "No. Signet has no server, no account, no telemetry, no analytics. It doesn't ask for the internet permission on Android. Your shared secrets live in your phone's secure enclave (Keychain/Keystore) and don't leave the device. If Signet disappeared tomorrow, nothing of yours goes with it.",
  "@faqA5": {"description": "FAQ answer 5."},

  "faqQ6": "What if I lose my phone?",
  "@faqQ6": {"description": "FAQ question 6."},

  "faqA6": "If you made a backup before losing it, you can restore the paired contact onto a new phone with \"Restore from backup\". Your counterparty doesn't need to do anything, and doesn't even know a restore happened. If you didn't back up, the pairing is gone and you'd need to re-pair in person with that contact on the new device.",
  "@faqA6": {"description": "FAQ answer 6."},
  "faqQ12": "How do I keep a backup safe?",
  "@faqQ12": {"description": "FAQ question about storing the two backup parts."},
  "faqA12": "A backup has two parts: a PACKAGE file, which is locked, and 8 words, which unlock it. Keep them apart. Save the PACKAGE file off the phone, for example on your computer or a USB stick. Write the 8 words on paper and keep the paper somewhere else, like your wallet. Anyone who finds both parts can impersonate you to your contacts, so never keep them together and never send the words in a message. To restore, tap Restore from backup, load the PACKAGE file, then type the 8 words.",
  "@faqA12": {"description": "FAQ answer about storing the two backup parts."},

  "faqQ7": "Can I pair with more than one person?",
  "@faqQ7": {"description": "FAQ question 7."},

  "faqA7": "Yes. Add as many as you want — each pairing is independent. Every relationship gets its own secret; compromising one pairing doesn't reveal or affect any other.",
  "@faqA7": {"description": "FAQ answer 7."},

  "faqQ8": "What's the challenge-response grid?",
  "@faqQ8": {"description": "FAQ question 8."},

  "faqA8": "An offline fallback for when the other person can't reach their phone. Both of you have the same 8x8 grid of code words derived from your shared secret. You say \"what's the phrase for orange-anchor?\" — they look it up (in the app or on a printed card) and read you the three-word answer. If it matches what your app says, the pairing is genuine.",
  "@faqA8": {"description": "FAQ answer 8."},

  "faqQ9": "Why does video-call verify ask for a physical action?",
  "@faqQ9": {"description": "FAQ question 9."},

  "faqA9": "AI voice and video deepfakes keep getting better. A realtime deepfake that hears you ask for the 4 words can just say them — unless those words came from the paired device, which is why plain verify works. But a deepfake that hears you read a random prompt like \"touch your ear\" aloud can also mimic that action immediately. So Signet turns the action into something derived from the shared secret too: only the real paired device knows which action is expected in this window. When you turn on VIDEO CALL mode, passing requires BOTH the right 4 words AND the counterparty performing the expected action. A deepfake without the paired device can only guess, and the combined odds drop to roughly 1 in 100 trillion per 30-second window.",
  "@faqA9": {"description": "FAQ answer 9. 'VIDEO CALL' refers to the on-screen toggle label."},

  "faqQ10": "Why no account? What if I need recovery?",
  "@faqQ10": {"description": "FAQ question 10."},

  "faqA10": "An account means a server, a password, and a path an attacker (or a subpoena) can use to reach your pairings without touching your phone. Signet exists specifically because those paths exist for every other auth tool. Recovery is manual: make a backup now, while things are calm, and keep it somewhere you can reach if you lose the phone.",
  "@faqA10": {"description": "FAQ answer 10."},

  "faqQ11": "Someone is asking me to skip the verify step.",
  "@faqQ11": {"description": "FAQ question 11."},

  "faqA11": "Do not skip it. A real paired contact understands why verification exists and won't pressure you to bypass it. Urgency plus a request to skip verification is the exact pattern a scammer uses to short-circuit the safety net. If you're being pressured, assume the call is hostile until proven otherwise.",
  "@faqA11": {"description": "FAQ answer 11."},

